WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:05.520
If I was to host a hypothetical&nbsp;
head-to-head tournament to determine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:09.720
the most evil serial killer of all&nbsp;
time, who do you think would win?

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:15.840
The safe money would be on history's homicidal&nbsp;
heavy hitters - Jack the Ripper, Ted Bundy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:22.320
Jeffery Dahmer. But if you had to bet your entire&nbsp;
life savings on one individual, you could do a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:29.460
lot worse than back the man often referred to&nbsp;
as America's first serial killer - HH Holmes.

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:34.500
During a reign of sheer terror in Chicago&nbsp;
in the late 19th century, Holmes is thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:39.480
to have brutally murdered more than 200&nbsp;
people at a rate of more than 40 per year.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:45.240
Many of his victims met their maker in a house&nbsp;
of death that came to be known as the murder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:51.840
castle. Built to a labyrinthine plan that only&nbsp;
Holmes himself understood, the sprawling complex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:58.080
was full of secret passages, trap doors, and&nbsp;
soundproofed torture chambers. Holmes lured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:04.140
hundreds of innocent people to this morbid mansion&nbsp;
with murder on his mind, and after killing them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:09.600
in the most horrific ways imaginable, some were&nbsp;
dissected, others were stripped of their flesh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:16.200
and sold to medical schools as skeletons, and&nbsp;
still others were melted in giant vats of acid.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:21.960
When you take into account the volume and sheer&nbsp;
brutality of his crimes, it's hard to imagine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:28.920
anyone can rival HH Holmes for the title of the&nbsp;
most evil serial killer ever to have lived...

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:32.040
...but there's a catch. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:36.780
almost nothing I've told you about&nbsp;
HH Holmes so far is actually true.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:42.660
Don't get me wrong, I didn't just make it up.&nbsp;
Every single detail - the sky-high body count,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:49.020
the castle of death, the dissections and&nbsp;
torture chambers - it's a matter of public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:54.240
record. The staggeringly gory story&nbsp;
is the subject of newspaper articles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.080
encyclopaedia entries, and multiple documentaries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:05.160
Which makes it all the more strange&nbsp;
that the entire thing is utter nonsense.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:13.260
This is the confusing tale of HH Holmes,&nbsp;
America's most misunderstood murderer.

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:44.040
Holmes was born under the rather less enigmatic&nbsp;
epithet of Herman Webster Mudgett on the 16th of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:49.800
May 1861 in the town of Gilmanton, New Hampshire.&nbsp;
Almost nothing is known about his early years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:54.960
Some sources claim he had an abusive alcoholic&nbsp;
father and that his favourite childhood pastime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.960 --> 00:04:00.120
was to torture the local wildlife, but those&nbsp;
are just rumours with no documented evidence.

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:05.040
Mudgett married at the age of just 17 to the&nbsp;
first of what would eventually be 4 wives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:08.220
though since he never bothered actually divorcing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:13.680
any of them we can add bigamy to&nbsp;
his soon-to-be long list of crimes.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:18.240
Mudgett was never a particularly strong student,&nbsp;
but he managed to scrape his way to a medical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:23.160
degree from the University of Michigan, and it&nbsp;
was there that we hear rumours of his earliest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:27.900
crimes. Despite his reputation as one of&nbsp;
America's most notorious serial killers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:34.740
Mudgett's first felonious forays didn't&nbsp;
involve *creating* cadavers, but selling them.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:39.360
In those days, corpses were a&nbsp;
surprisingly valuable commodity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:45.480
sold on the black market to medical schools for&nbsp;
anatomical research. And Mudgett was only too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:50.160
happy to dig up the deceased from the local&nbsp;
cemeteries in order to make some fast cash.

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:55.620
...Or at least, that's what several sources claim.&nbsp;
Again, there's no real evidence to prove he was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.620 --> 00:05:00.660
bodysnatcher, but that probably shouldn't come as&nbsp;
much of a surprise. I'm no criminal mastermind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:06.060
but if I was in the business of selling&nbsp;
corpses, I probably wouldn't write receipts.

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:10.380
We *do* know that body snatching was&nbsp;
relatively commonplace at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:15.960
and Michigan university was a bit of a hotspot.&nbsp;
There's also the probably-not-coincidental fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:21.720
that after his execution some years later, Holmes&nbsp;
was so worried someone might come along and dig&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:27.480
up his remains that he asked to be buried in&nbsp;
a block of solid concrete 10 feet underground.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:33.060
So yeah, there's a fair chance this&nbsp;
particular Holmes rumour is actually true.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:38.520
After graduating in 1884, Mudgett worked&nbsp;
several odd jobs across the American northeast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:43.560
gaining a reputation as a bit of&nbsp;
a womaniser and a lot of a conman.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:50.400
Two years later he moved to Chicago, where&nbsp;
he restyled himself under the now infamous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:57.540
pseudonym of Dr. Henry Howard Holmes - HH&nbsp;
Holmes to his mates. Most historians agree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.540 --> 00:06:02.640
the name change was designed to stop the various&nbsp;
people he'd swindled from tracking him down, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:07.200
I‘m guessing he was probably also just a bit sick&nbsp;
of having a name like a squib from Harry Potter.

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:12.660
According to the traditional tale, on arriving&nbsp;
in Chicago he found work at a local pharmacy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:16.740
where he eventually murdered his boss&nbsp;
to seize control of the business.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:25.140
In truth, Mudgett - well, Holmes - bought&nbsp;
the drugstore fair and square. Far from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:30.240
being Holmes' victims, the previous&nbsp;
owners outlived him by several decades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:36.060
a fact that's proven by the dates on their&nbsp;
headstones which still survive to this day.

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:41.940
Between the pharmacy and a growing list&nbsp;
of illegal activities, Holmes was fast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:47.580
becoming a wealthy man. So he did what any of&nbsp;
us would do with a bit of spare cash - he bought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:53.700
an empty plot of land and started work on a&nbsp;
giant death factory (we've all been there).&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.700 --> 00:07:00.780
This grand building you see before you, known&nbsp;
today as the 'murder castle,' was the result.

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:07.020
Now, if you happen to be especially observant,&nbsp;
you may already have noticed that this infamous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:13.080
'castle' looks suspiciously like a fairly&nbsp;
standard mixed use commercial building.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:17.160
And it turns out there's a pretty good&nbsp;
reason for that - that's exactly what it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:22.980
was. There were shops on the ground floor&nbsp;
and apartments above. Holmes added a third&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:27.600
storey in 1892 which he planned to use&nbsp;
as a hotel, but it was never finished.

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:35.160
In 1893 Chicago played host to a World's Fair&nbsp;
- a 6-month bonanza that attracted a staggering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:41.700
27 million visitors. Probably the most famous&nbsp;
element of the commonly told HH Holmes story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:46.500
is that he built his murder castle to take&nbsp;
advantage of this huge influx of tourists.

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:54.840
In the era before people posted 450 social media&nbsp;
updates every single day, tourists were vulnerable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.840 --> 00:08:01.080
- their friends and relatives often had little&nbsp;
idea of exactly where they were at any one time.

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:08.280
So, according to the story, Holmes built his&nbsp;
murdery mansion as a way of ensnaring large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:15.060
numbers of hard-to-trace World’s Fair visitors&nbsp;
- preferably young women ripe for murdering.

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:21.540
That all sounds fairly logical - well, if you’re&nbsp;
a serial killer - but it turns out that Holmes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.540 --> 00:08:28.920
started building his 'castle' several years before&nbsp;
the Chicago World's fair had even been planned.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:34.260
Admittedly work on the third floor did roughly&nbsp;
coincide with the announcement of the Fair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:39.720
but we can be fairly certain he didn't&nbsp;
lure any young women there for Netflix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:43.980
and dismemberment because the hotel&nbsp;
part was never actually finished.

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:50.340
Several people were indeed murdered on the&nbsp;
premises - more on that in a minute - but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:56.700
whole 'murder castle' concept is total nonsense.&nbsp;
The original architectural plans for the building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.700 --> 00:09:03.360
don't show any secret passageways, trap doors, or&nbsp;
purpose-built torture chambers, and the building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:10.200
simply wasn't big enough to have enclosed&nbsp;
the complex labyrinth later accounts claimed.

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:15.300
Almost all these wild stories came from the&nbsp;
press after Holmes was finally brought to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:19.380
justice a few years later. It might sound&nbsp;
unlikely that journalists could have gotten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:24.660
the details of the case so incredibly wrong,&nbsp;
but Holmes' capture happened to coincide with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:30.660
the heyday of what's now known as yellow&nbsp;
journalism - a style of reporting where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:36.720
newspapers just made everything up to sell&nbsp;
more copies. Why does that sound familiar?

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:41.940
But while there's no evidence the building&nbsp;
was some kind of murder metropolis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:48.360
it *was* used as the base of operations for a&nbsp;
wide range of less gruesome cons and schemes.

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:53.400
To put it simply, if HH Holmes believed&nbsp;
something could make him money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:58.920
he was already doing it. During his criminal&nbsp;
career he's known to have forged checks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:04.500
stolen horses, bought expensive items on credit&nbsp;
with no intention of ever paying off the loans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:10.440
and defrauded a wide variety of insurance&nbsp;
firms in multiple ingenious ways.

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:14.460
But while he wasn't the mass murdering&nbsp;
maniac most people think he was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.460 --> 00:10:20.220
Holmes was still a killer - and a pretty&nbsp;
prolific one at that. We know for sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:25.980
that he murdered 4 people, and there's a&nbsp;
good chance there were at least 9 victims.

00:10:25.980 --> 00:10:31.380
The first is thought to have been Julia Connor,&nbsp;
the wife of one of Holmes' employees, Ned.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:37.140
Holmes and Julia started an affair in&nbsp;
the early 1890s, and when Ned found out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:41.400
he quite understandably quit his job&nbsp;
and moved away. Not long afterwards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:46.380
Julia and her daughter Pearl went&nbsp;
missing, never to be seen again.

00:10:46.380 --> 00:10:51.540
We don't know for sure what happened to them, but&nbsp;
Holmes later claimed Julia had fallen pregnant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:57.180
and that she'd died when he attempted to&nbsp;
perform an illegal abortion. It's generally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.180 --> 00:11:01.980
assumed he killed Pearl because she knew too&nbsp;
much about the circumstances of her mother's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:08.160
death and therefore represented a dangerous&nbsp;
loose end. Julia's remains were never found,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:14.160
but the body of a girl of about Pearl's age&nbsp;
was dug up in Holmes' cellar after his arrest.

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:19.800
5 months after Julia and Pearl went missing,&nbsp;
Holmes struck again in incredibly similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:26.220
circumstances - he started a relationship with one&nbsp;
of his employees - Emeline Cigrande - before she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:32.160
suddenly went missing. Once again, Holmes&nbsp;
claimed a botched abortion was to blame.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:36.960
In retrospect that man probably&nbsp;
shouldn’t have had a medical licence.

00:11:38.760 --> 00:11:43.200
The following year two more women&nbsp;
with ties to Holmes disappeared,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:48.960
Minnie and Nannie Williams. As with all the&nbsp;
others, the details are sketchy, but it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:53.520
thought Holmes scammed them out of some property,&nbsp;
then murdered them before they had a chance to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:59.820
go to the police. Much like Julia Connor and&nbsp;
Emeline Cigrande, their bodies were never found.

00:11:59.820 --> 00:12:05.580
There are plenty more stories about people turning&nbsp;
up at Holmes' 'castle' never to be seen again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:09.900
and several of them are quite possibly true.&nbsp;
But convincing evidence is extremely thin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:14.820
on the ground and it's likely we'll never&nbsp;
know exactly how many people Holmes killed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:21.780
though there’s zero chance it was&nbsp;
anywhere near the commonly circulated 200.

00:12:21.780 --> 00:12:28.740
By 1894, he'd swindled (and murdered) so many&nbsp;
people in Chicago that he decided it was probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:34.500
best to move on. He travelled first to Fort&nbsp;
Worth, then St. Louis, and it was there that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.500 --> 00:12:40.440
law finally caught up with him. Considering he was&nbsp;
very probably a serial killer at this point, It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:46.440
almost laughable that Holmes was jailed for the&nbsp;
heinous crime of failing to pay off store credit.

00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:52.140
Remarkably it was his first stint in jail,&nbsp;
and while he didn't stay behind bars for long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.140 --> 00:12:56.400
his brief incarceration kicked&nbsp;
off a chain of events that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.400 --> 00:13:00.360
eventually lead to him being hanged for&nbsp;
murder... but I'm getting ahead of myself.

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:05.160
While in jail, Holmes met a man&nbsp;
by the name of Marion Hedgepeth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:11.520
better known as the Handsome Bandit. Handsome&nbsp;
Hedgepeth was a Wild West gunslinger who was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.520 --> 00:13:18.780
serving 25 years in the slammer for train robbery.&nbsp;
Both being colossal bastards, Holmes and Hedgepeth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.780 --> 00:13:24.180
soon struck up a friendship, and between them they&nbsp;
cooked up a scam with a potentially huge payoff:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.780 --> 00:13:27.300
Holmes was going to fake his own death.

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:32.760
If you're wondering how pretending to die&nbsp;
could possibly be a get rich quick scheme,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:35.520
I've got two words for you: Life Insurance.

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:40.860
Holmes was bailed not long afterwards and&nbsp;
he promptly put the plan into action. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.860 --> 00:13:43.020
don't know exactly how he staged his own death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.020 --> 00:13:46.860
but we do know the insurance company&nbsp;
became suspicious and refused to pay out.

00:13:46.860 --> 00:13:51.900
Holmes was undeterred. He knew he could pull&nbsp;
the scam off if only he could make things a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.900 --> 00:13:56.820
little more convincing. But faking your&nbsp;
own death isn't like fighting Malenia in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.820 --> 00:14:03.300
Elden ring - you can't keep trying over&nbsp;
and over and over and over and over and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.300 --> 00:14:07.980
over.... again. And that meant Holmes&nbsp;
needed to find someone else who was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.980 --> 00:14:12.060
willing to fake his own death in return&nbsp;
for a slice of the life insurance pie.

00:14:12.060 --> 00:14:16.920
That man was his long time business&nbsp;
associate and literal partner in crime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.920 --> 00:14:20.460
Benjamin Pitezel, and their&nbsp;
plan was pretty simple.

00:14:20.460 --> 00:14:24.660
Step 1: Take out a fat life&nbsp;
insurance policy in Benjamin’s name

00:14:25.200 --> 00:14:28.860
Step 2: Get hold of a dead body&nbsp;
of about his size and build

00:14:28.860 --> 00:14:32.580
Step 3: Blow said body up in a planned explosion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.580 --> 00:14:37.380
making sure it gets good and burned in the&nbsp;
process to conceal its original features.

00:14:37.380 --> 00:14:39.480
Step 4: Report the death

00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:41.640
Step 5: …Profit

00:14:41.640 --> 00:14:46.200
The only question was, how to make sure the&nbsp;
insurance company wouldn't see through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.200 --> 00:14:51.660
ruse. Holmes had already discovered just how&nbsp;
hard they were to fool… but that experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.660 --> 00:14:57.060
gave him an idea - what if he didn't&nbsp;
have to fool them in the first place?

00:14:57.720 --> 00:15:01.800
As you've probably already guessed,&nbsp;
rather than risk using a random corpse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.800 --> 00:15:07.620
Holmes simply murdered Benjamin Pitezel&nbsp;
so he could make the claim for real.

00:15:07.620 --> 00:15:12.300
Pitezel’s wife had been in on the scam from&nbsp;
the start - she was the one who needed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.300 --> 00:15:17.608
file the claim with the insurance firm - but&nbsp;
she had no idea her husband had been double&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.608 --> 00:15:22.200
crossed. She went through the entire claims&nbsp;
process - including identifying the charred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:28.200
remains of her dearly beloved - all while&nbsp;
believing he was really hiding out in London.

00:15:29.280 --> 00:15:33.060
Of course, there was only so long that&nbsp;
deception could last - sooner or later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:38.640
Mrs Pitezel and her children were going to&nbsp;
want to see their husband and father again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.640 --> 00:15:42.360
But Holmes wasn’t worried - by&nbsp;
this point in his career as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:46.560
professional arsehole he had plenty&nbsp;
of experience tying up loose ends…

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:52.680
Along with Benjamin, to keep his secret safe&nbsp;
Holmes murdered 3 of the 5 Pitezel children.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:57.840
Two of the girls - Alice and Nellie - were&nbsp;
forced into a large trunk which Holmes filled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.840 --> 00:16:03.720
with asphyxiating gas, while one of the boys -&nbsp;
Howard - was poisoned, dismembered, and burned.&nbsp;

00:16:03.720 --> 00:16:09.240
The Pitezel family killings are the only Holmes&nbsp;
murders we can be sure he committed - not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.240 --> 00:16:13.560
did he confess to the crimes after his&nbsp;
arrest, he gave detailed descriptions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.560 --> 00:16:18.900
of how and where they took place, and he also&nbsp;
told police where they could find the remains.

00:16:18.900 --> 00:16:24.120
Thankfully, those were the final murders HH&nbsp;
Holmes would ever have the chance to commit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.120 --> 00:16:28.200
Because his old prison pal the Handsome&nbsp;
Bandit had been doing some talking.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.980 --> 00:16:33.420
It turns out Holmes had bragged about&nbsp;
all manner of evil deeds while locked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.420 --> 00:16:37.980
up with Handsome Hedgepeth, and the train&nbsp;
robber had decided to use that information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.980 --> 00:16:43.380
to buy himself an early release. He&nbsp;
told the police everything he knew.

00:16:43.980 --> 00:16:51.120
Holmes was arrested in Boston on the 17th of&nbsp;
November 1894. Despite his numerous crimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:57.000
a relative lack of evidence meant he was tried&nbsp;
solely for the murder of Benjamin Pitezel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.000 --> 00:17:03.360
But that alone was more than enough to see him&nbsp;
given the death sentence. On May the 7th 1896,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:09.300
HH Holmes was hanged. When the trap was&nbsp;
sprung his neck didn't break, and he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.300 --> 00:17:15.120
left twitching at the end of the rope for a full&nbsp;
20 minutes before finally being pronounced dead.

00:17:15.120 --> 00:17:21.600
As I've already mentioned, we have some highly&nbsp;
questionable 19th century journalism to thank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.600 --> 00:17:25.560
for many of the more outrageous&nbsp;
stories told about HH Holmes today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.560 --> 00:17:30.420
but it’s worth pointing out that Holmes himself&nbsp;
is responsible for much of the confusion.

00:17:30.420 --> 00:17:35.580
Before his death, Holmes admitted to&nbsp;
killing 27 people. The confession,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.580 --> 00:17:40.500
delivered straight from the homicidal&nbsp;
horse's mouth, should have been pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.500 --> 00:17:45.780
solid evidence. But things were never that&nbsp;
simple when it came to HH Holmes. Not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.780 --> 00:17:49.740
did Holmes’ version of events contain&nbsp;
several contradictions and revisions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.740 --> 00:17:54.120
he also claimed to have killed several people&nbsp;
who turned out to still be very much alive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.120 --> 00:17:59.100
and several others who were known to have&nbsp;
died of natural causes hundreds of miles away.

00:17:59.100 --> 00:18:04.620
As for what his motive was for confessing&nbsp;
to crimes he didn't commit, your guess is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.620 --> 00:18:08.400
as good as mine. Some people believe&nbsp;
he was deliberately trying to fan the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.400 --> 00:18:13.440
flames of his own legend before his death,&nbsp;
while others think he was trying to confuse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:13.440 --> 00:18:18.840
the prosecution during his trial. Perhaps he&nbsp;
was just a few sandwiches short of a picnic.

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:26.100
Even now, almost 130 years after his death,&nbsp;
mystery continues to swirl around HH Holmes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:32.340
Along with the widespread misinformation about&nbsp;
his life and deeds, a decades-long conspiracy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.340 --> 00:18:38.280
theory claimed that Holmes didn’t really die&nbsp;
in 1896. Instead, according to the theory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:38.280 --> 00:18:44.160
he pulled off one last trademark scam,&nbsp;
escaping his own execution by convincing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:44.160 --> 00:18:50.100
someone else to ‘take the fall’ in his place.
The rumour gained so much backing that in 2017,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.100 --> 00:18:54.300
his remains were exhumed from their&nbsp;
concrete tomb for examination.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.840 --> 00:19:02.100
Thankfully, genetic testing proved conclusively&nbsp;
that the one thing HH couldn’t cheat was death.

00:19:02.940 --> 00:19:04.080
Thanks for watching.

